gradients: [('The prompt may have led to incorrect predictions because it lacks specificity in defining what constitutes a "lie." The instruction to simply determine if the statement is a lie or not may not provide enough guidance for the classifier to evaluate the context, nuance, and factual accuracy of the statements effectively. Additionally, the use of "Yes" and "No" responses without clear examples or criteria could confuse the model, as it may struggle to discern the subtleties of truthfulness versus misleading statements. Providing clearer criteria or examples of what qualifies as a "lie" versus a "non-lie" could improve the accuracy of the predictions.', '## Example 1\nText: "Statement: Of our 98,000 teachers who are K-12, over 53,000 of those teachers today are over 50 years old.\nJob title: Governor\nState: Virginia\nParty: democrat\nContext: a speech."\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 2\nText: "Statement: Says Scott Brown co-sponsored legislation to let employers deny women coverage for birth control.\nJob title: Senator\nState: New Hampshire\nParty: democrat\nContext: a news release"\nLabel: No\nPrediction: Yes\n\n## Example 3\nText: "Statement: Obama\'s budget plan would take national debt up to about 80 percent of gross national product. ... Historically, it\'s been about 40 percent.\nJob title: U.S. Senator\nState: \nParty: republican\nContext: a Fox News interview"\nLabel: No\nPrediction: Yes\n\n## Example 4\nText: "Statement: On military recruiters at Harvard, Elena Kagan "took a position and the Supreme Court ruled unanimously that she was wrong."\nJob title: Senator\nState: Wyoming\nParty: republican\nContext: an interview on Fox News"\nLabel: No\nPrediction: Yes')]
